# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Scheduler — crontab entries
#
# Install for the user that owns the app (NOT root unless the app
# runs as root):
#     crontab -e          # then paste the block below
#     crontab -l          # confirm it took
#
# The old in-app scheduler ran inside every uvicorn worker, so
# `--workers 2` fired the same job twice a night. One cron entry
# on one host replaces that entirely.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Cron runs with a nearly empty environment: no PATH beyond a bare
# default, no virtualenv, and NOT the login shell's variables. Set
# what the job needs explicitly rather than assuming.
SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin

# Cron's own timezone is the system's. Keep the system clock in the
# same zone as [scheduler] timezone in scheduler.conf, or a run just
# after midnight can be for the previous day. Check with
# `timedatectl` (or `date`) before trusting the hour below.

# ── Option A: one entry, the master scheduler (simplest) ──────
# Runs every due task in one sweep, in the right order, with each
# task isolated from the others' failures.
0 2 * * * cd /opt/shopmanagement && /opt/shopmanagement/.venv/bin/python -m scheduler.run_scheduler master >> /var/log/shopmanagement/scheduler.log 2>&1

# ── Option B: one entry per task (schedule them separately) ───
# Each script connects, checks the settings, runs only its own
# task and records the result in the same ledger. Use this when
# tasks want different times or frequencies. Do NOT use both
# options at once - pick one, or the same occurrence gets picked
# up by whichever fires first (harmless, since every task is
# idempotent, but it makes the run log confusing).
#
# 55 1 * * * cd /opt/shopmanagement && /opt/shopmanagement/.venv/bin/python -m scheduler.run_future_task_checker >> /var/log/shopmanagement/scheduler.log 2>&1
#  0 2 * * * cd /opt/shopmanagement && /opt/shopmanagement/.venv/bin/python -m scheduler.run_rent_generation    >> /var/log/shopmanagement/scheduler.log 2>&1
#  5 2 * * * cd /opt/shopmanagement && /opt/shopmanagement/.venv/bin/python -m scheduler.run_due_date_penalty   >> /var/log/shopmanagement/scheduler.log 2>&1
#
# Run the future-task checker FIRST if you split them up: it is what
# registers upcoming runs and spots ones that were missed.

# Notes
# -----
# * Adjust the two paths above: the directory CONTAINING scheduler/,
#   and the python interpreter. The scheduler is standalone - it needs
#   only its own four packages (pip install -r scheduler/requirements.txt),
#   so it can have its own virtualenv, or share the app's. Either works.
# * The scheduler writes its OWN per-task log files - scheduler/logs/
#   rent_generation.log, due_date_penalty.log, future_task_checker.log,
#   master.log and errors.log - rotated daily and gzipped. The `>> ... 2>&1`
#   above is therefore optional; it only captures the same lines a second
#   time. Keep it if you want one combined file for cron, or set
#   `console = false` under [logging] in scheduler.conf and drop the
#   redirect entirely.
# * To put the log files somewhere else (a log mount, /var/log), set
#   `dir` under [logging] in scheduler.conf or export SCHEDULER_LOG_DIR.
# * Exit codes: 0 ran, 1 failed, 2 skipped (disabled), 3 bad usage.
#   Anything non-zero other than 2 is worth alerting on. Note a
#   FAILED task still exits 0 - it is recorded in the ledger and
#   retryable from the Scheduler dashboard, which is where failures
#   belong rather than in cron mail.
# * The on/off switches live in the Scheduler settings screen (in the
#   database), NOT in scheduler.conf - that file only says how to reach
#   the database, what timezone the schedule is in, and where to log.
#   Turning the master switch off leaves cron running while every due
#   task records itself as skipped.
# * Safe to run more than once a day — a tenant/shop already billed
#   for the month is skipped, and concurrent runs are serialised by a
#   database lock.
#
# Docker: a container usually has no cron daemon, and adding one is
# more trouble than it is worth. Two options.
#
# Against the API container (it already ships scheduler/):
# 0 2 * * * docker exec shopmanagement-api python -m scheduler.run_scheduler master
#
# Or as its own container - the scheduler is standalone, so it needs
# nothing from the API image beyond this folder and four packages:
# 0 2 * * * docker run --rm --env-file /opt/tms/.env tms-scheduler python -m scheduler.run_scheduler master
